</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From these standard artifacts, we systematically generate two sets of conflicting evidence:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reversed Artifacts (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The reversed model (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) was created by manually reversing all sequential dependencies in <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reversed log (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) was generated by reversing the event order in each trace of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, the reversed description (<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) was created by manually adjusting <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> to match the new process flow of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shuffled Artifacts (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> The shuffled model (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) was created by applying a random, bijective mapping of activity labels to the standard model <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving its control-flow structure. The shuffled log (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) was created by applying the same mapping to the activity names in <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding description (<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) was then derived manually.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This setup provides us with six distinct input scenarios for the LLM: three based on text descriptions (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) and three based on event logs (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>). To compactly represent event logs as textual input for LLMs, we generate a textual abstraction for each log using the process mining library PM4Py <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the conflicting artifacts, <a href="#S3.F2.sf4" title="2(d) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Artifact Generation ‣ 3 Evaluation Methodology ‣ Knowledge-Driven Hallucination in Large Language Models: An Empirical Study on Process Modeling" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(d)</span></a> shows the standard, shuffled, and reversed models for the sales order process. The corresponding reversed description (<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the textual abstraction of the reversed event log (<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>) are provided in <a href="#LST1" title="Listing 1 ‣ 3.1 Artifact Generation ‣ 3 Evaluation Methodology ‣ Knowledge-Driven Hallucination in Large Language Models: An Empirical Study on Process Modeling" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#LST2" title="Listing 2 ‣ 3.1 Artifact Generation ‣ 3 Evaluation Methodology ‣ Knowledge-Driven Hallucination in Large Language Models: An Empirical Study on Process Modeling" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
